WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.658
What if you could teach a spacecraft to land on the Moon with just thirty-six numbers?

00:00:05.658 --> 00:00:08.142
No neural network, and no gradients.

00:00:08.142 --> 00:00:09.741
Only evolution.

00:00:10.341 --> 00:00:12.117
This is Lunar Lander.

00:00:12.117 --> 00:00:15.977
The goal is to touch down softly between the two flags.

00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:21.116
Gravity pulls the lander down, and every burst of an engine costs fuel.

00:00:21.716 --> 00:00:25.122
At every step, the lander senses eight numbers.

00:00:25.122 --> 00:00:32.543
Where it is, how fast it is moving, how it is tilted and spinning, and whether each leg touches the ground.

00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:36.823
And at every step, it chooses one of four actions.

00:00:36.823 --> 00:00:42.769
Do nothing, fire the left engine, fire the main engine, or fire the right engine.

00:00:43.369 --> 00:00:45.910
Every flight happens on a new moon.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:50.754
The mountains are random, and the lander gets a random shove at the start.

00:00:50.754 --> 00:00:53.831
So the pilot can't memorise one flight.

00:00:54.431 --> 00:00:58.782
But the landing pad is always in the middle, at the same height.

00:00:58.782 --> 00:01:05.127
The lander measures its position from the pad, so the goal is always the same: get to zero, zero.

00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:11.711
Each pilot is tested on ten random moons, so evolution has to find a rule that works everywhere.

00:01:12.311 --> 00:01:15.285
The pilot is one small table of numbers.

00:01:15.285 --> 00:01:21.232
Each of the eight inputs is multiplied by a weight for each action, and a bias is added.

00:01:21.232 --> 00:01:23.452
That gives four scores.

00:01:24.052 --> 00:01:26.935
The action with the highest score fires.

00:01:26.935 --> 00:01:30.778
Watch the scores rise and fall as the lander flies.

00:01:31.378 --> 00:01:36.192
Eight times four weights, plus four biases, makes thirty-six numbers.

00:01:36.192 --> 00:01:41.056
Finding good values for them is the job of the genetic algorithm.

00:01:41.656 --> 00:01:44.966
We start with a population of random pilots.

00:01:44.966 --> 00:01:48.513
Each one is just thirty-six random numbers.

00:01:49.113 --> 00:01:52.152
Every pilot flies, and gets a fitness score.

00:01:52.152 --> 00:01:53.789
Random pilots crash.

00:01:53.789 --> 00:01:57.537
Some of them crash a little less badly than others.

00:01:58.137 --> 00:01:59.885
The best half are kept.

00:01:59.885 --> 00:02:03.400
They decide where the next generation comes from.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.715
That's the whole loop.

00:02:05.715 --> 00:02:10.576
Sample a population, fly every pilot, rank them, and move toward the winners.

00:02:10.576 --> 00:02:11.719
Then repeat.

00:02:11.719 --> 00:02:13.663
Nothing is differentiated.

00:02:13.663 --> 00:02:16.708
The algorithm only ever sees the scores.

00:02:17.308 --> 00:02:20.103
The algorithm we use is called CMA-ES.

00:02:20.103 --> 00:02:25.145
Here it is on a toy problem with only two numbers, so we can watch it work.

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:28.240
Dark means good, and bright means bad.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.181
It keeps a cloud of candidates, shaped like a stretched bell curve.

00:02:33.181 --> 00:02:39.328
Each generation it samples new points, keeps the best half, and moves the centre toward them.

00:02:39.928 --> 00:02:42.793
It also learns the shape of the problem.

00:02:42.793 --> 00:02:49.240
The cloud stretches along the valley, races down it, and shrinks as it closes in on the answer.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.722
How do we score a pilot?

00:02:51.722 --> 00:02:56.427
It flies ten episodes, and we start from its average reward in the game.

00:02:56.427 --> 00:02:59.962
That already punishes crashing and wasted fuel.

00:03:00.562 --> 00:03:05.434
Then we add a big bonus for landing, and a small one for landing quickly.

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:10.728
And one penalty: every engine burst after both legs are down costs half a point.

00:03:10.728 --> 00:03:13.975
A good pilot lands, and then stays still.

00:03:14.575 --> 00:03:16.708
Now let's watch it learn.

00:03:16.708 --> 00:03:22.242
Each dot is one pilot, and each column is one generation of fifty pilots.

00:03:22.842 --> 00:03:27.276
In the first twenty generations, nearly every pilot still crashes.

00:03:27.276 --> 00:03:34.166
But the dots keep creeping upward, because each generation is sampled around the best of the last one.

00:03:34.766 --> 00:03:37.194
Then the whole population lifts.

00:03:37.194 --> 00:03:40.836
By generation fifty, more than half the pilots land.

00:03:40.836 --> 00:03:43.690
By seventy-five, almost all of them do.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:52.124
The orange line is the best pilot, the blue line is the average, and the gap closes as the population settles on one strategy.

00:03:52.724 --> 00:03:55.299
Here is the same story in flight.

00:03:55.299 --> 00:04:02.506
All fifty pilots of one generation start from exactly the same spot, and we draw the path of each one.

00:04:03.106 --> 00:04:05.195
Generation zero is chaos.

00:04:05.195 --> 00:04:08.804
Pilots fall straight down, or fly off the screen.

00:04:08.804 --> 00:04:12.523
Five generations later, not one of them lands.

00:04:13.123 --> 00:04:16.255
By generation twenty, three pilots make it.

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:17.975
By thirty, fourteen.

00:04:17.975 --> 00:04:19.879
By forty, twenty-seven.

00:04:19.879 --> 00:04:23.741
And by fifty, thirty-six of the fifty pilots land.

00:04:24.341 --> 00:04:26.523
By the end, all fifty land.

00:04:26.523 --> 00:04:33.183
The whole population flies almost the same path, a smooth curve down to the middle of the pad.

00:04:33.783 --> 00:04:36.425
This is the population as points.

00:04:36.425 --> 00:04:42.782
Each pilot is thirty-six numbers, flattened down to two, so each dot is a whole pilot.

00:04:43.382 --> 00:04:49.684
The centre of the cloud travels a long way from where it started, toward the region where pilots land.

00:04:49.684 --> 00:04:51.989
The white line is its path.

00:04:52.589 --> 00:04:54.627
And there's a neat trick here.

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:59.883
Only the highest score matters, so making every weight bigger doesn't change any decision.

00:04:59.883 --> 00:05:04.817
The weights grow about a hundred times bigger, while the step size stays around one.

00:05:04.817 --> 00:05:11.070
So each step gets smaller and smaller compared with the weights, and the search turns into fine-tuning.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:16.148
Here is the centre of the cloud, drawn as the weight matrix itself.

00:05:16.148 --> 00:05:18.477
At generation zero it is noise.

00:05:18.477 --> 00:05:20.747
Watch the pattern appear.

00:05:21.347 --> 00:05:24.993
The strongest rules are about tilt, spin and drift.

00:05:24.993 --> 00:05:28.569
Exactly the things a human pilot would watch.

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:35.085
Here are the best pilots from different points in training, all facing the same start.

00:05:35.685 --> 00:05:40.698
The random pilot flies away, and the champion of generation ten crashes.

00:05:40.698 --> 00:05:47.139
From generation thirty on, the best pilot lands, and each one lands a little more cleanly.

00:05:47.739 --> 00:05:51.828
Because the pilot is so simple, we can follow every decision.

00:05:51.828 --> 00:05:54.198
Here the lander is falling fast.

00:05:54.198 --> 00:05:57.457
Each input times its weight adds to each score.

00:05:57.457 --> 00:06:04.157
The falling speed pushes the main engine up and do nothing down, and the main engine wins by a nose.

00:06:04.757 --> 00:06:08.589
And here it has just landed, with both legs on the ground.

00:06:08.589 --> 00:06:14.454
The leg switches add big votes to do nothing, so do nothing wins, and the lander stays still.

00:06:14.454 --> 00:06:17.831
That's the rule the settle penalty taught it.

00:06:18.431 --> 00:06:20.754
We can draw a whole rule at once.

00:06:20.754 --> 00:06:23.927
Across is the tilt, and up and down is the spin.

00:06:23.927 --> 00:06:27.456
Each colour is the action the pilot would choose.

00:06:28.056 --> 00:06:30.370
The white line is a real flight.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:34.593
Tilt or spin one way, and it fires the engine that turns it back.

00:06:34.593 --> 00:06:41.835
The border between them runs right through the middle, so the pilot keeps itself upright with small corrections.

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:44.397
So what did evolution find?

00:06:44.397 --> 00:06:46.471
The main engine is the brake.

00:06:46.471 --> 00:06:53.106
Falling speed has the biggest weight on it, so the faster the lander falls, the harder it wants to brake.

00:06:53.706 --> 00:06:56.038
Do nothing is the landing rule.

00:06:56.038 --> 00:06:59.866
Each leg that touches the ground adds a big vote for it.

00:06:59.866 --> 00:07:03.933
With both legs down, it wins, and the engines go quiet.

00:07:04.533 --> 00:07:07.291
The left engine fixes drift and tilt.

00:07:07.291 --> 00:07:13.541
It fires when the lander drifts right, sits right of the pad, tilts right, or spins clockwise.

00:07:13.541 --> 00:07:16.170
And it pushes the lander back.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.633
And the right engine is its mirror image.

00:07:19.633 --> 00:07:25.302
Drifting left, tilting left, or spinning the other way, and the right engine pushes back.

00:07:25.302 --> 00:07:28.407
Evolution found the symmetry on its own.

00:07:29.007 --> 00:07:35.520
Tested on one hundred new landings it never saw in training, this pilot lands ninety-five times.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.450
And once it's down, it stays down.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:43.376
All of it from thirty-six numbers and a loop of trial and error.

00:07:43.376 --> 00:07:45.178
The code is on GitHub.

00:07:45.178 --> 00:07:48.362
Run it, and watch your own pilot evolve.
